---
import CodeBlock from "../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro";
import ModelSection from "../../components/models/ModelSection.astro";
import ModelLayout from "../../layouts/ModelLayout.astro";
import { sectionLookup } from "../../lib/models";

const sections = [
  {
    id: "how-they-work-together",
    title: "How they work together",
  },
  {
    id: "models-at-a-glance",
    title: "Models at a glance",
  },
  {
    id: "example-inputs-and-outputs",
    title: "Example inputs and outputs",
  },
  {
    id: "quickstart",
    title: "Quickstart",
  },
  {
    id: "provenance",
    title: "Base models and data",
  },
  {
    id: "limitations",
    title: "Limitations",
  },
  {
    id: "license-and-citation",
    title: "License and citation",
  },
];
const section = sectionLookup(sections);
---

<ModelLayout slug="nouli-cyber-specialists" sections={sections}>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Turn vulnerability descriptions and threat-report sentences into structured answers for
      security workflows. The CVSS specialist returns a vector, score and triage route; the
      ATT&amp;CK specialist suggests technique labels.
    </p>
    <p class="release-scope">
      Run either specialist on CPU or CUDA. The optional source selector requires CUDA and a
      separate public base download.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <ModelSection {...section("how-they-work-together")}>
    <p>
      <img
        src="/models/nouli-cyber-specialists/how-it-works.svg"
        alt="Vulnerability descriptions go to the CVSS specialist for metrics, scoring and triage. Threat-report sentences go to the ATT&amp;CK specialist for technique suggestions, with optional source selection first."
      />
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the specialist that matches your task. CVSS predicts eight base metrics; code calculates
      the score, severity and example triage route. ATT&amp;CK suggests techniques supported by
      report text. The optional selector chooses original sentences before ATT&amp;CK mapping. Nouli
      can connect these answers to rules and record the resulting workflow.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("models-at-a-glance")}>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Component</th>
          <th>Input → output</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>CVSS triage</td>
          <td>Vulnerability description → CVSS v3.1 vector, score, severity and route</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ATT&amp;CK mapping</td>
          <td>Behavior description → probabilities and suggestions across 49 technique labels</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Optional source selector</td>
          <td>Report sentences → references to selected original sentences</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The decision specialists run locally on CPU or CUDA. The optional selector requires CUDA and a
      separate public base download. No hosted API key is required.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("example-inputs-and-outputs")}>
    <h3>CVSS triage</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Input:</strong> “A remote unauthenticated attacker can exploit the vulnerability
      without user interaction to read or modify application data.”
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Recorded output</strong> (probabilities and trace fields omitted):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Recorded output"
      code={`{
  "vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N",
  "base_score": 8.2,
  "severity": "HIGH",
  "route": "prioritize"
}
`}
    />
    <p>
      The bundled routing rule prioritizes high or critical findings with network access, no
      privileges and no user interaction. Adapt that rule to your workflow.
    </p>
    <h3>ATT&amp;CK mapping</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Input:</strong> “The malware logs keystrokes, takes screenshots, collects files from
      the local system and exfiltrates the collected data over its existing command-and-control
      channel.”
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Recorded output</strong> (probabilities and trace fields omitted):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Recorded output"
      code={`{
  "threshold": 0.45,
  "techniques": ["T1005", "T1041", "T1056.001", "T1113"]
}
`}
    />
    <p>
      These correspond to Data from Local System, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel, Keylogging and
      Screen Capture. Both examples were run with the supplied weights on CPU.{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-cyber-specialists/tree/main/examples">
        Complete inputs and outputs
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("quickstart")}>
    <p>
      Use Python 3.12 and the{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/huggingface_hub/guides/cli">HF CLI</a>. Download the
      complete repository:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      label="Terminal"
      code={`hf download nouli-ai/nouli-cyber-specialists --revision v1.0.0 --local-dir ./nouli-cyber-specialists
cd nouli-cyber-specialists
python3.12 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements-inference.txt
.venv/bin/python scripts/infer.py --bundle . --task cvss --device cpu \\
  --text "$(cat examples/cvss-input.txt)"
.venv/bin/python scripts/infer.py --bundle . --task tram --device cpu \\
  --text "$(cat examples/tram-input.txt)"
`}
    />
    <p>
      Keep the repository together: its checkpoints use the supplied custom loader. See{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-cyber-specialists/resolve/b7ef4f17e8040037001137e67928e8536955e81c/GETTING_STARTED.md">
        setup and Nouli integration
      </a>{" "}
      for CUDA, source selection, offline use and the native CVSS workflow.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("provenance")}>
    <p>
      CVSS uses <a href="https://huggingface.co/answerdotai/ModernBERT-base">ModernBERT</a>;
      ATT&amp;CK combines{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/cisco-ai/SecureBERT2.0-base">SecureBERT 2.0</a> and{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/ibm-research/CTI-BERT">CTI-BERT</a>. The optional selector is
      an adapter for{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507">Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Data sources are{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Tenebrae/cvss31vectors">CVSS31Vectors</a> and{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/ctokx/tram-attack-multilabel-clean">
        TRAM ATT&amp;CK Multilabel Clean
      </a>
      . Pinned source revisions and notices accompany the release.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("limitations")}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        English text only; suggestions need analyst review. These models do not inspect code or
        establish that a system is secure.
      </li>
      <li>
        CVSS estimates are not authoritative vulnerability scores. ATT&amp;CK coverage is limited to
        the 49 bundled labels; an empty result does not mean benign behavior.
      </li>
      <li>
        Long inputs are truncated to the documented limits. Source selection is optional and can
        omit useful evidence.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ModelSection>
  <ModelSection {...section("license-and-citation")}>
    <p>
      Apache-2.0; upstream and data notices apply. See{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-cyber-specialists/resolve/b7ef4f17e8040037001137e67928e8536955e81c/NOTICE">
        NOTICE
      </a>{" "}
      and{" "}
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/nouli-ai/nouli-cyber-specialists/tree/main/licenses">
        licenses
      </a>
      . CVSS is owned by FIRST and used by permission. ATT&amp;CK® is a registered trademark of The
      MITRE Corporation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://nouli.ai">Nouli AI</a>. Nouli Cyber Specialists. Cite the published version
      and commit.
    </p>
  </ModelSection>
</ModelLayout>
